The desk merged different date problems into #4471. Its "known, one issue, one fix" summary
does not match the report groups below. This proposal replaces the subject-only rule in
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/). That accepted record remains unchanged.

<Callout kind="warn">
This triage policy is a proposal for the support desk. The report export ends on 4 October 2026.
The course has since published the engineering outcomes below. Keep those outcomes separate from the historical report counts.
</Callout>

## Engineering update after the export

The [challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) now reports that #4471 closed on 5 October 2026.
The parser is **fixed**, with the change described as `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
[Parser ticket #1](https://github.com/timkrase/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) is closed as completed.

The hint issue is **won't fix** because the `booking-v3` form was retired.
[Hint ticket #2](https://github.com/timkrase/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) is closed as not planned.
Retiring the form does not mean its hint was repaired. These outcomes come from the course's engineering update,
not from testing production code in this export.

## Two problems were merged together

<div class="cause-table" role="region" aria-label="Cause counts, scroll horizontally on small screens" tabindex="0">
  <CauseTable ticket={4471} />
</div>

The parser group owns #4471 because it is the largest group. At export time, the hint group had no ticket of its own.
The submitted GitHub issues document each problem separately, with report IDs, counts, and the final outcomes above.
The table describes the historical export, before those outcomes.

### Parser reads day-first dates as month-first

The shared parser always reads month first. In a day-first tenant, an ambiguous date such as
`07/04/2026`, intended as 7 April, becomes 4 July. App and email reports use this path.
Form reports from tenants on `booking-v2` also belong here because its `DD/MM/YYYY` hint agrees with their locale.

R-41200 from Harbour Dental gives the 7 April to 4 July example. R-41205 is another parser report,
even though intake guessed `hint` from an `en-US` browser. Its tenant, Morrow Tax, uses `fr-FR` and `booking-v2`.

The required parser behavior is to preserve the tenant's date convention or accept an explicit unambiguous representation.
The acceptance checks proposed by this investigation cover round trips for ambiguous day-first dates across app, email, and booking-v2 inputs.
Include a month-first control and a day above 12 to catch fallback behavior. The export contains the desk's
classifier, not the production parser, so these are proposed acceptance checks rather than a shipped patch.

### Booking-v3 prints the wrong hint for day-first tenants

`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form reads dates in the tenant's day-first order.
A person follows the hint and types `01/06/2026` for 6 January. The form reads 1 June.
R-41230 reports that result at Copper Studio. Its browser is `en-GB` and intake guessed `parser`,
but the tenant runs `booking-v3`, so this is a hint report.

This problem affects form submissions from Alder Physio, Copper Studio, and Pale Harbour.
App and email reports from those same tenants still belong to the parser group.
While booking-v3 was in service, the hint and the form's interpretation needed to agree with the tenant locale.
The proposed acceptance checks required a person following the displayed hint to get the intended date on booking-v3,
with booking-v2 and month-first controls. A parser-only fix would not correct the form's misleading hint.
The desk chose to retire this form rather than fix it.

## Counts come from the export, not the intake tags

<IncidentReview />

The tenant directory supplies locale and form version. `dayFirst` asks `Intl.DateTimeFormat` for the date order.
`causeOf` uses that order and the report's channel. `uniqueById` removes overlapping export rows before grouping.
Browser locale, intake tags, subject text, and the size of a ticket are not evidence that two reports share a cause.

## The queue was not one incident morning

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received time measures incoming reports. Merged time measures desk work. A bulk merge must not be read as a sudden
increase in failures. The continued arrivals after July show that marking the ticket known did not stop the reports.
The graph ends at the export boundary and makes no claim about later desk activity.

## Proposed decision: merge only a confirmed shared cause

Replace the subject-only instructions on the [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) with this workflow after desk review.

1. Count each report ID once. Read the body and look up the tenant, its locale, its form version, and the channel.
2. Use `causeOf` and compare with the existing ticket's cause. Merge confirmed matching causes even when their subjects differ.
3. Keep different causes apart even when subjects match. Give each cause its own engineering ticket and count.
4. If the tenant or cause is unknown, keep the report separate for investigation. Two unknown causes are not a confirmed match.
5. Keep conflicting symptoms in the ticket as follow-up evidence. Reclassify only when the investigation supports it.

<DedupeExplorer />

Kettle Books app and email reports have different subjects but the same parser cause.
Alder Physio form and app reports can have identical subjects but different causes.
Copper Studio and Morrow Tax can submit the same form subject yet have different causes because their form versions differ.
Pale Harbour and Alder Physio form reports can have different subjects but the same hint cause.
The unknown-sender example stays separate even when both subjects match.

## Consequences and review

Cause-based triage requires a tenant lookup and a body read. It gives engineering separate work for separate defects
and keeps ticket totals tied to report IDs. Preserve the old decision as history, flag contradictory old guidance,
and link this proposal from the incident and triage pages. Review the proposal with the support desk before adopting it.

The KB index and page frame calculate review age. A recent review date does not prove the prose is correct:
compare its claims with the live tables, examples, and queue. Rebuild after report or tenant changes so the desk sees current values.

